            146 => 
            array (
                'id' => 1460,
                'lang_code' => 'en',
                'name' => 'Islamic Treasury & Risk Management Products',
                'description' => '<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>Islamic Finance Training presents a course that aims to analyze treasury and risk management products offered by Islamic Financial Institutions (IFIs) in various jurisdictions.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>Delegates will be led through the vocabulary and nomenclature of Islamic finance applicable to the Islamic treasury. Various Islamic toolkits will be examined to learn how they are applied in the &lsquo;money market&rsquo; and risk management activities.</p>
<p><br />Islamic Treasury &amp; Risk Management Products training course will also examine how Sukuk is relevant to Treasury activities &ndash; be it for liquidity management, long term funding options or capital adequacy purposes. It will &nbsp;also look at how the various Islamic tools are applied to manage forex and rate risks</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>',
                'duration' => '5',
            'days_content' => '["<p><strong>The Nature of Money &amp; Implication on Treasury<br /><br /></strong></p>\\n<ul>\\n<li>The rules of Sarf and contract</li>\\n<li>Riba Al-Nasiah</li>\\n<li>Discussion: identifying assets that may be traded or</li>\\n<li>pledged from a Sharia&rsquo;a perspective</li>\\n<li>Overview of traditional Money Market activities:</li>\\n<li>Deposit &amp; Interbank transactions</li>\\n<li>Tradable Instruments</li>\\n<li>Repo</li>\\n</ul>\\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\\n<p><strong>Key Islamic Depository Tools<br /><br /></strong></p>\\n<ul>\\n<li>Summary rules for non-remunerative deposits:</li>\\n<li>Qard/Wadiah deposits</li>\\n<li>Summary rules for remunerative deposits:</li>\\n<li>Mudarabah deposits</li>\\n<li>&nbsp;Wakalah deposits</li>\\n<li>Murabaha deposits</li>\\n<li>Traditional Intrabank Relationship: Fund Transfer Pricing</li>\\n<li>(FTP), Inter branch Pricing (IBP) etc</li>\\n</ul>","<h6>Main Islamic Interbank Tool</h6>\\n<h6>&nbsp;</h6>\\n<p>●&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp; Murabaha vs. Tawarruq vs. Reverse Murabaha - is there any difference?<br />●&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp; Shari\'a concerned about this tool<br />●&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp; Brokers, OTC and live warrants<br />●&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp; Breakage, Back value and compounding<br />●&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp; Suq al Sila - Murabaha and Tawarruq in Malaysia<br />●&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp; Standardized documents: IIFM, AIBIM<br />●&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp; Leakage of Islamic funds - what is this? How do different markets deal with this?</p>\\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\\n<h6>Other Islamic Interbank Tool</h6>\\n<p><br />●&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp; Wakalah to do Murabaha vs. Investment Wakalah - what&rsquo;s the difference?<br />●&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp; Case study: Investment Dar vs. Blom Interbank Wakalah<br />●&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp; Interbank Mudarabah<br />●&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp; Wadiah for hibah &amp; Wadiah for points</p>","<p><strong>Tradable Instruments</strong><br /><br /></p>\\n<ul>\\n<li>A brief review of traditional tradable tools:</li>\\n<li>Certificate of Deposits</li>\\n<li>Government Securities (T-Bills etc)</li>\\n<li>Banker&rsquo;s Acceptance</li>\\n<li>Commercial Papers</li>\\n<li>Quotation basis: yield vs. discount</li>\\n<li>Does the Islamic market have alternatives for all the tools above?</li>\\n</ul>\\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\\n<p><strong>Introduction to Sukuk - alternative Islamic tradable instruments</strong></p>\\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\\n<ul>\\n<li>Short term, high grade issuance for Basel III:</li>\\n<li>Central Bank of Bahrain Salam Sukuk</li>\\n<li>Short term Sukuk Ijarah</li>\\n<li>Are these instruments freely tradable in the secondary</li>\\n<li>market?</li>\\n<li>Longer-Term Sukuk</li>\\n<li>Common structures: Ijarah, Istithmar</li>\\n<li>Asset-based vs. Asset-backed Sukuk</li>\\n<li>Tradability - Shari\'a guideline</li>\\n<li>Tenure: Malaysia vs. Global</li>\\n<li>Pricing: Fixed-rate vs. Floating Rate Sukuk</li>\\n</ul>\\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\\n<p><strong>Repo<br /><br /></strong></p>\\n<ul>\\n<li>&nbsp;Understanding a conventional repo</li>\\n<li>&nbsp;Why are Repos not widely used in the Islamic market?</li>\\n<li>How could an Islamic repo work?</li>\\n<li>The IIFM solution</li>\\n<li>Alternative solutions</li>\\n</ul>","<h6>Managing FX Risk&nbsp;</h6>\\n<p><br />● Shari\'a compliant forward alternatives:<br />&raquo; Promise based solutions - unilateral promise, two<br />unilateral promises vs. bilateral promises<br />&raquo; Promise to deposit i.e. Cross Currency Deposit<br />&raquo; Are these solutions on the balance sheet or off-balance<br />sheet?<br />&raquo; Exiting these forward?<br />● How do Islamic banks hedge themselves?<br />&raquo; Long Murabaha<br />&raquo; Back to back promise<br />Group Exercise: Examine a traditional FX Swap &amp; construct a<br />Sharia&rsquo;a compliant FX Swap</p>\\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\\n<h6>Managing FX Risk II</h6>\\n<h6>&nbsp;</h6>\\n<p>● Sharia&rsquo;a tools that provide options:<br />&raquo; Arbun<br />&raquo; Promises<br />&raquo; Juala<br />● How do we use the above to structure Shari\'a compliant<br />currency options?<br />&raquo; Arbun - is the arbun for currency?<br />&raquo; Promises - how do we structure the option premium?<br />&raquo; Which solution is widely used?<br />● Calculating effective and break-even strike rate<br />● Are the options tradable?<br />Group Exercises:<br />1. How to use Tawarruq for Currency Option?<br />2. Are the following FX Risk Management Strategies<br />replicable in the Islamic market?<br />i. Put Option<br />ii. Put Spread</p>","<h6>Managing Rate Risk&nbsp;</h6>\\n<h6>&nbsp;</h6>\\n<p>● Shari\'a analysis of the following traditional tools for interest<br />rate risk management<br />&raquo; Forward Rate Agreement (FRA)<br />&raquo; Interest Rate Futures<br />&raquo; Swaps - Interest Rate Swap (IRS) and Cross Currency<br />Swap (CCS)<br />&raquo; Interest Rate Options<br />Group Discussion: Are the following Interest Rate Option<br />Strategies replicable in the Islamic market?<br />i. Interest Rate Cap<br />ii. Interest Rate Floor<br />iii. Interest Rate Collar</p>"]',
                'related_courses' => '[1138,179,1140,1168]',
                'category_id' => 34,
                'online' => 0,
                'created_at' => '2025-04-03 12:49:13',
                'updated_at' => '2025-04-03 12:49:13',
            ),
