ments</p>\\n\\t</li>\\n\\t<li>\\n\\t<p>Types of fires and their effect on people and equipment</p>\\n\\t</li>\\n\\t<li>\\n\\t<p>Types of explosions and their effects on people and equipment</p>\\n\\t</li>\\n\\t<li>\\n\\t<p>Review the available programs for the calculations of the results</p>\\n\\t</li>\\n</ul>\\n","<p><strong>The role of quantitative risk assessment QRA</strong></p>\\n\\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\\n\\n<ul>\\n\\t<li>\\n\\t<p>Introduction to Quantitative Risk Assessment (QRA)</p>\\n\\t</li>\\n\\t<li>\\n\\t<p>The role of event tree analysis in scenario development</p>\\n\\t</li>\\n\\t<li>\\n\\t<p>The role of fault tree analysis for multi-cause analysis</p>\\n\\t</li>\\n\\t<li>\\n\\t<p>Requests for ETA and FTA Fault Tree Analysis</p>\\n\\t</li>\\n\\t<li>\\n\\t<p>Data on failure to use in QRA&#39;s quantitative risk assessment</p>\\n\\t</li>\\n\\t<li>\\n\\t<p>Societal and individual risks</p>\\n\\t</li>\\n\\t<li>\\n\\t<p>Review of available programs for quantitative risk assessments</p>\\n\\t</li>\\n\\t<li>\\n\\t<p>Return the report on the fifth day and discuss it</p>\\n\\t</li>\\n\\t<li>\\n\\t<p>Program review and introduction</p>\\n\\t</li>\\n</ul>\\n"]',
